  "notes": [
    "Machine CPU is the GetSystemTimes busy share over the workload alone. A capture\u0027s start (profile compilation, session enablement, its group mask) and its stop, rundown, last publication and checkpoint are outside it; its live publications every 5 s are inside it.",
    "Each capture is the product\u0027s own icat record process, recording into a scratch session that is deleted once its counters are read.",
    "A profile\u0027s cost is the median over rounds of its busy share minus the same round\u0027s no-capture share; signed differences are kept, and only the class clamps a negative median at zero.",
    "A profile\u0027s throughput regression is the median over rounds of the workload\u0027s runs a second against the same round\u0027s no-capture run, a faster run counting none, as OverheadClassCalculator reads one; the signed slowdown is kept beside it.",
    "syncObjectsOverExplore is the same medians between the two captures: what the opt-in adds to Explore on this workload.",
    "A capture\u0027s observed and admitted rates are over the span its sources delivered in, from its first record to its last: the whole capture, longer than the workload, so they are averages, not the workload\u0027s peak. The handle group delivers every type\u0027s handles machine-wide, which its gate omits all but the synchronization types\u0027 of.",
    "applicationDrops are the records InterCat\u0027s own full queue dropped, and sourceLoss what ETW reported lost at a provider or in its buffers: different quantities, kept apart (\u00A79.3). A capture with either states the gap in its coverage; its cost figures are then those of a capture that did not keep every record.",
    "captureProcessCpuPercentagePoints is the icat record process\u0027s own processor time over the same window, as a share of the whole machine. It excludes the kernel\u0027s and the workload\u0027s own threads\u0027 work of raising events, so it is a lower bound on the capture\u0027s cost."
  ]
